                              THE BRAID



   “Se oye,” she said. You can hear it. “Ahora sabes por qué pagan por
los parlantes.”
   Now you know why they pay for the speakers.
   Eliseo fed everybody afterward, because that’s what a gagá leader is
for. A pot of chivo guisado came out of somebody’s kitchen, then rice,
then a bucket of ice with Presidentes in it, and plastic plates went hand
to hand across the yard. Wilfredo’s uncle, still in the sequined dress,
carried a plate to every person sitting down before he took one himself.
Nobody made a joke about it, which is how I knew he did it every year.
   The old woman with the trumpet came and sat in the chimney’s
shade, one chair away from Abuela. Up close she was older than I’d
thought, with gray braids pinned under a red head wrap and a burn
scar across the back of one hand. She ate without talking. So did
Abuela. They watched the young drummer show a little girl how to
hold the sticks.
   Then the old woman reached over with her fork, took the best piece
of goat off her own plate, and put it on Abuela’s.
   Abuela looked at the goat. She looked at the woman. She took the
soft piece of yuca from the bottom of her own plate, the piece every‐
body fights over, and put it on the woman’s.
   Neither of them mentioned it.
   “Mi abuela cortaba caña aquí,” the old woman said after a while,
and tipped her chin at the chimney. Her Spanish had Kreyòl running
under it, a second current under the first. “Desde chiquita. Ella decía
que antes, en el corte, se cantaba.”
   Before, in the cane, people sang.
   “Mi mamá limpiaba las oficinas,” said Abuela. “En la capital. Calle
Las Damas.”
   The old woman nodded slowly. She looked at Abuela’s hands, and
Abuela looked at hers, at the scar, and neither of them explained any‐
thing to me.
   “Mèsi pou ti gason an,” the old woman said, and lifted her chin in
my direction.
   “She says thank you for the boy,” said Abuela.
   “Tell her I just pulled a plug.”



